<worksheet xml:id="bayes-theorem">
    <title>Bayes' Theorem (1.5)</title>
    <page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                There are three bowls:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Bowl <m>1</m> ( <m>B_{1}</m> ) contains <m>2</m> red and <m>4</m>
          white chips
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Bowl <m>2</m> ( <m>B_{2}</m> ) contains <m>1</m> red and <m>2</m>
          white chips
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Bowl <m>3</m> ( <m>B_{3}</m> ) contains <m>5</m> red and <m>4</m>
          white chips
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    Suppose a chip is chosen as follows: Choose a bowl, choose a chip. Further
    suppose the probability of choosing each bowl is as follows:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>P(B_{1}) = \frac{1}{3}</m>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <m>P(B_{2}) = \frac{1}{6}</m>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <m>P(B_{3}) = \frac{1}{2}</m>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
          
        </introduction>
        <task workspace=".25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the probability of getting a red chip given that bowl 1
    was chosen ( <m>P(R \mid B_{1})</m> )? (recall this is conditional
    probability)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace=".25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   What is the probability of getting a red chip given that bowl 2
    was chosen?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace=".25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   What is the probability of getting a red chip given that bowl 3
    was chosen?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task> 
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   What is the probability of getting a red chip?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
         <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   <alert>Now suppose we know that a red chip was drawn.</alert> Does knowing
    the outcome of the experiment change any of these probabilities? Which bowl
    do you think the chip most likely came from?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

 
</page>
<page>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                We would like to compute the
    <em>conditional probability that bowl three was chosen given the outcome of the experiment was red</em>:
    <m>P(B_{3} \mid R)</m>. This probability is called the
  </p>

  <p>
    <fillin characters="90"/>  
  </p>
</statement>
    </exercise> 
    <exercise workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
    <alert>Bayes' Theorem</alert> These ideas generalize: Suppose that events
    <m>B_{1},B_{2},...,B_{m}</m> are mutually exclusive and exhaustive (Recall
    that this means that their union is the whole sample space and no two sets
    overlap) and <m>A</m> is an event:
  </p>

  <image xml:id="bayes-theorem-image-1" margins="20%">
    <latex-image> 
    \begin{tikzpicture} 
    \draw (-4,-1) rectangle (1,1);
    \draw (-3,1)--(-3,-1)
    (-2,1)--(-2,-1)
    (-1,1)--(-1,-1)
        (0,1)--(0,-1);
     \draw (-1.5,-.1) ellipse (2cm and .75cm);
     \draw (-3.5,.75) node[font=\tiny] {$B_1$}
     (-2.5,.75) node[font=\tiny] {$B_2$}
           (-1.5,.75) node[font=\tiny] {$B_3$}
           (-.5,.75) node[font=\tiny] {$\cdots$}
           (.5,.75) node[font=\tiny] {$B_m$}
           (-1.5,0) node[font=\tiny] {$A$};
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>

  <p>
    Then any event A can be written as a union of mutually exclusive sets:
  </p>
  </statement>
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
             At a hospital emergency room patients are classified and it is found
          that <m>20\%</m> are classified as "critical'', <m>30\%</m> are
          classified as "serious'' and <m>50\%</m> are classified as
          "stable''. Of those classified as critical, <m>30\%</m> die, of those
          classified as serious, <m>10\%</m> die, and of those classified as
          stable, <m>1\%</m> die. Given that a patient dies, what is the
          probability that they were classified as critical?
        </p>
        <p>
          Let C = classified critical; S = classified serious; St=classified
          stable; D=patient dies
        </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
 <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
              During a blackout <m>100</m> people are arrested on suspicion of
          looting. Each person takes a polygraph test. Suppose it is known that
          the results of the polygraph are <m>90\%</m> reliable when
          administered to a guilty person and <m>98\%</m> reliable when given to
          an innocent person. Further suppose we know that the probability that
          any given suspect is guilty is <m>0.12</m>. A random suspect is tested
          and the polygraph result says that the person is guilty. Find the
          probability that the suspect is actually innocent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let PG = polygraph predicted guilt; PI = polygraph predicted
          innocence; G = the person is guilty; I = the person is innocent
        </p>
    </statement>
 </exercise>
 </page>
       <page>
 <exercise workspace="3in">
 <statement>
    <p>
          It rains a lot in Oregon. Of the last 365 days, it was cloudy or rainy
          in the morning on 220 of those days and sunny in the morning on the
          others. Of the days that started out cloudy or rainy, it rained for
          more than 3 hours during the day on 150 of those days. Of the days
          that started out sunny, it rained for more than 3 hours during the day
          on 50 of those days. Suppose we choose one of the last 365 days at
          random and find that it rained more than 3 hours that day. What is the
          conditional probability that the day started out sunny?
    </p>
 </statement>
 </exercise>
     <exercise workspace="1.5in">
     <statement>
        <p>
            Assume that a teacher of a Spanish class knows the following
          correlation between High School GPA and the probability of getting an
          A in their class. Further they know what fraction of their class had a
          particular HS GPA.
        </p>
         <p>
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ccc}\hbox{HS GPA}&amp;\hbox{Fraction of Class} &amp; \hbox{Probability of A}\\ \hline3.0 - 4.0 &amp; 0.50 &amp; 0.75\\ 2.0-2.99 &amp; 0.35 &amp; 0.50\\ \hbox{Below} 2.0 &amp; 0.15 &amp; 0.25\end{array}
          </md>
          Given that a particular student got an A, what is the probability that
          the student's high school GPA was in the 3.0-4.0 range?
        </p>
     </statement>
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A certain disease is known to occur in 1 in 1000 people. A test for
              the disease indicates the presence of the disease (a positive test) 95% of the time when the disease 
              is present and the presence of the disease 4% of the time when the disease is not present. 
              
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            This test is given to a random person in the population and they test positive for the disease. 
              What is the probability that they actually have the disease?
          </p>
        </statement>
        </task>
         <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            This test is given to a random person in the population and they test negative for the disease. 
              What is the probability that they actually do not have the disease?
          </p>
        </statement> 
        </task>
    </exercise>
        </page>
        
  </worksheet>